---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "nameservers",
 "name": "Google Cloud DNS",
 "title": "How to Change Nameservers in Google Cloud DNS",
 "description": "Change nameservers in Google Cloud DNS step by step: where the setting lives, what values to enter, and the propagation window to expect.",
 "intro": "Nameserver changes are delegation changes: you're telling the internet which servers hold your domain's records. Here is exactly where Google Cloud DNS keeps that setting and what to expect.",
 "panel": "The zone detail page shows the assigned nameservers (ns-cloud-XX.googledomains.com plus partners). Delegate at the registrar to these.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Copy the NS set",
   "Zone details: Registrar setup section shows the exact nameservers."
  ],
  [
   "Delegate",
   "Set them at the registrar as custom nameservers."
  ],
  [
   "Confirm",
   "NS lookup shows the ns-cloud set; Google marks the zone as visible when delegation lands."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Zones live inside a GCP project: people edit the wrong project's zone constantly. Also the registrar-setup block is the authoritative NS list, not guesswork.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Pricing?",
   "Per managed zone and per million queries; free tier covers modest traffic. Billing lives in the GCP project."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Google Cloud DNS's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for Google Cloud DNS to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in Google Cloud DNS, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
